From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Bates <stephen.bates@amd.com>
Date: 2026-07-30
Subject: [PATCH 10] fix: make DEVICE_TYPE=cpu an unconditional opt-in

lmcache/v1/platform/_detect_device() advertises DEVICE_TYPE as a forced
device selection, and CpuDeviceSpec's own docstring calls DEVICE_TYPE=cpu
"the supported path for forcing CPU selection".  It is not reachable:
the branch is gated on spec.is_available(), and CpuDeviceSpec
deliberately inherits the base-class False (documented invariant -- the
auto-detection loop's `if spec.device_type == "cpu": continue` is the
paired defence).  So DEVICE_TYPE=cpu logs "not available or not
registered" and falls through to auto-detection.

That is harmless when auto-detection is honest.  It is not harmless when
torch.cuda.is_available() lies -- which is exactly the AIC emulation
case: vllm_emulator's cuda_mock shims torch.cuda.is_available() to True
on a CPU-only node so vLLM's GPU code paths run, and CudaDeviceSpec.
is_available() is a bare torch.cuda.is_available().  LMCache then selects
CUDA, resolve_kv_wrapper_factory binds the CUDA IPC wrapper, and
create_transfer_context() tries to export device handles for KV tensors
that live in host memory.

Honour the opt-in without the availability probe.  Note the returned
module is StubCPUDevice, not torch.cpu: torch.cpu has no
Event/Stream/current_stream, which is the surface LMCacheMPConnector's
start_load_kv()/wait_for_save() call through torch_dev.  This is the same
object the no-accelerator tail fallback already returns.

The auto-detection loop and the CpuDeviceSpec.is_available() invariant
are untouched, so nothing changes when DEVICE_TYPE is unset.

diff --git a/lmcache/v1/platform/__init__.py b/lmcache/v1/platform/__init__.py
index f498b2b..35989c2 100644
--- a/lmcache/v1/platform/__init__.py
+++ b/lmcache/v1/platform/__init__.py
@@ -89,6 +89,23 @@ def _detect_device() -> tuple[Any, str]:
     if env_device_type is not None:
         env_device_type = env_device_type.strip().lower()
         spec = _DEVICE_REGISTRY.get(env_device_type)
+        if env_device_type == "cpu" and spec is not None:
+            # Explicit CPU opt-in, which the docstring of ``CpuDeviceSpec``
+            # already calls "the supported path for forcing CPU selection" --
+            # but gating it on ``spec.is_available()`` made it unreachable,
+            # because that class deliberately inherits the base-class False.
+            # DEVICE_TYPE=cpu therefore fell through to auto-detection, and on
+            # a host where ``torch.cuda.is_available()`` lies (an emulated /
+            # mocked accelerator, or CUDA visible but unusable) auto-detection
+            # picks CUDA and every later dispatch assumes device handles that
+            # the CPU tensors cannot produce.  Honour the opt-in without the
+            # availability probe; ``StubCPUDevice`` -- not ``torch.cpu`` -- is
+            # the module that implements the stream/event surface callers use.
+            # First Party
+            from lmcache.v1.platform.cpu.stub_cpu_device import StubCPUDevice
+
+            logger.info("DEVICE_TYPE=cpu: forcing the CPU device (StubCPUDevice)")
+            return StubCPUDevice("cpu"), "cpu"
         if spec is not None and spec.is_available():
             torch_module = getattr(torch, spec.torch_module_name, None)
             if torch_module is not None:
